  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nPrevious AM update: \n12Z guidance continues to hone in on scattered discrete cells firing\noff across the Tri State after 3 PM today then congealing into more \nlinear segment. While some severe wind gusts are possible, forecast \nDCAPE and CAPE values are not robust. \n\nThere is a conditional tornadic threat later today, particularly for \nthe Tri-State, but much will hinge on how much clearing we have \nafter the AM rain showers. Large scale cyclonic motion/ascent over \nthe area, bulk shear around 35-40 knots, and 0-1 SRH values between \n100 to 150 m2/s2 near the Tri-State all suggest that isolated \ntornadic activity is possible. Similarly, machine learning such as \nNadocast and CSU echo this threat with 2-5% contours across \nsoutheastern Indiana and portions of the Tri-State. However, as \nmentioned previously, forecast instability appears more limited, \nwith only a wedge of 1000-1500 J/kg of SBCAPE moving into the Tri-\nState this evening. Much will depend on a dry slot pivoting through/ \nhow much cloud clearing we can advect in. \n\nOutside of the severe threat, we do still have concerns for locally \nheavy rainfall. While this morning's 12z balloon sampled PWATs just \nabove the 75th percentile, as the low pivots, additional moisture \nwill be advected in this afternoon and evening. For areas that have \nskinny CAPE present, high PWATs, and  the possibility for both slow \nmoving and training storms, localized flooding remains a concern. \n\n--\n\n\nA stagnant pattern remains in place through the rest of the weekend \nas the cut off low aloft remains situated right over the Tri-State, \nkeeping the ILN CWA on the northern side of the low. This will \ncontinue to result in low clouds, periods of rain or drizzle, and \noccasional thunderstorms.\n\nIsolated AM rain showers will transition to scattered thunderstorms \nby Sunday afternoon, aided by diurnal processes. As the low opens up \ninto an open wave, deep layer shear becomes more splotchy in nature \nacross eastern Ohio however, MLCAPE values should once again climb \nto 1000 - 1500 J/kg, which should support a low end damaging wind \nthreat with stronger/ more organized storms in the eastern OH/ KY \ncorridor. Highest PWAT values should shift off to the east, however, \nwith training storms some localized flooding concerns may still \narise.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nThe surface low will finally be pulled off to the northeast, pulling\nin some drier air from the northwest down into the Great Lakes\nregion. However, aloft, the open wave trough continues to linger\nacross the Tennessee and Ohio Valley region, with deeper moisture\nstretched from the Gulf through the Appalachians. This keeps periods\nof showers and thunderstorms in the forecast primarily for areas\nsoutheast of I-71 through Monday and into Tuesday. Temperatures are\nforecast to remain slightly below average, with highs only reaching\nthe low 80s.\n\nBy mid-week, long range guidance suggests that the trough will\nfinally be picked up by the more progressive northern jet stream and\nget carried off to the east. However, with its departure arrives a\nseries of shortwaves from the west, continuing our active pattern.",
